<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">3.1 Which route fits which buyer</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Choose authorized distribution if</strong> you need stock now and your customer asks for the brand. You are a reseller, not a brand owner.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Choose ODM stock private label if</strong> you want to test a market at MOQ 500 with the lowest risk.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Choose ODM design development if</strong> the stock model is too generic. You get a modified platform and custom packaging without a full mold.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Choose full OEM if</strong> your product needs a proprietary look or thermal design. Budget for tooling and a longer cycle.</p>
<blockquote class="border-l-4 border-brandOrange pl-4 italic text-slate-600 mb-4"><strong>EXPERT INSIGHT</strong>  "The mistake I see most often is a buyer choosing full OEM before testing demand. Start with 500 units of ODM stock, validate the listing, then move to custom tooling when the sell-through data supports it. You will spend less and learn more."  — Nina Nico, Global Procurement & Sourcing Manager at WOWOHCOOL, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/nico-power-bank-chargers" target="_blank" rel="noopener external">LinkedIn</a></blockquote>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">For MOQ, payment and delivery terms, see our <a href="/b2b-ordering-terms/">B2B ordering terms</a>. For the production sequence behind a private-label launch, read the <a href="/blog/power-bank-private-label-oem-production/">power bank private label production guide</a>.</p>
</div>
