<section id="what-anker-level-means-in-an-rfq" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">5. What "Anker-Level" Means in an RFQ</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">"Anker-level" is not a spec. It is a set of verifiable requirements. Put these in the RFQ and ask for evidence, not adjectives.</p>
<div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl mb-6"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse border border-slate-200 rounded-xl overflow-hidden"><thead><tr class="bg-brandBlue text-white text-badge font-black uppercase tracking-widest"><th class="p-4 text-left">Category</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Must specify</th><th class="p-4 text-left">Evidence to request</th></tr></thead><tbody class="text-slate-600"><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Cell</td><td class="p-4">Chemistry, capacity, grade, model number, supplier</td><td class="p-4">Cell datasheet, UN38.3 report naming the cell model, IEC 62133-2</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Power</td><td class="p-4">PD 3.0/3.1 revision, PPS, EPR, per-port allocation</td><td class="p-4">Protocol test report, load test at rated output</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Wireless</td><td class="p-4">Qi2 MPP or Qi2-compatible, magnet grade</td><td class="p-4">WPC certificate and authentication IC part number</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Safety</td><td class="p-4">Over-current, over-voltage, over-temperature, short circuit</td><td class="p-4">Protection circuit schematic and test log</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Certification</td><td class="p-4">CE, FCC, RoHS, UN38.3, IEC/UL 62368-1 where required</td><td class="p-4">Certificate number, issuing lab, scope covering the SKU</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Quality</td><td class="p-4">AQL level, aging regime, defect rate</td><td class="p-4">QC plan, aging log, batch traceability record</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-slate-100"><td class="p-4">Change control</td><td class="p-4">Written approval before cell or component change</td><td class="p-4">Contract clause with notification period and liability</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h3 class="text-lg font-black text-brandBlue mt-6 mb-3">5.1 The two details that matter most</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>First, the certificate must name the product and the cell.</strong> A CE or UN38.3 report that covers a "similar model" is not evidence for your SKU. Ask for the exact model and cell reference.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4"><strong>Second, the AQL number must be the factory's own plan.</strong> A supplier that quotes "AQL 2.5" as a buyer target may run a looser internal plan. Ask what they actually apply, and ask for the inspection record.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Our <a href="/blog/quality-control-guide/">quality control guide</a> explains how IQC, IPQC, FQC and OQC fit together. If you suspect rebuilt cells, the <a href="/blog/b-grade-rebuilt-cells-power-bank-oem-detection/">B-grade cell detection guide</a> shows what to test.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="the-2025-recall-lesson-and-the-7-point-scorecard" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-xl p-6 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-6">6. The 2025 Recall Lesson and the 7-Point Scorecard</h2>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">The CPSC recall is a useful case study because the facts are public and specific. Recall 25-338, announced June 12, 2025, covered about 1,158,000 PowerCore 10000 units, model A1263. CPSC recorded 19 reports of overheating, fire or explosion, two minor burns and more than $60,700 in property damage. The remedy was a free replacement.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">Anker's own recall page says the affected units were manufactured from January 2016 through October 2019. The remedy requires proof of purchase or a photo of the marked unit, plus confirmation of proper lithium-battery disposal.</p>
<p class="text-slate-600 leading-relaxed mb-4">In June 2025, Anker also confirmed that a cell supplier had made an unauthorized material change, and said it had ended cooperation with that supplier. That action was separate from the CPSC A1263 recall above. 36Kr later put Anker's 2025 power bank recalls worldwide at about 2.38 million units. At its annual shareholder meeting reported in May 2026, Anker said it had cut roughly 70% of charging SKUs.</p>
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto mb-8">
