<section id="faq" class="mb-16">
<div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-6">
<div class="bg-slate-50 rounded-2xl p-8 border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
<h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-brandBlue uppercase italic mb-8 text-center">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
<div class="space-y-6 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Who manufactures Anker products?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Anker is a brand, not a private-label factory. It does not publish which contract manufacturers build its products, and it does not offer a public private-label program at 500 units. Its OEM arm works with large retail partners under private agreements. That is why "who makes Anker" is the wrong sourcing question. The same factory can build a reliable unit for one brand and a rebuilt-cell unit for another. Ask for the cell model, certificate scope and change-control clause.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Can I rebrand Anker products?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">No. Unauthorized rebranding is trademark infringement. It also breaches the distribution agreement and exposes you to injunctions and damages. Authorized resale is legal, but the product keeps Anker's brand, packaging and pricing rules. If you need your own logo, the legal route is an ODM program from 500 units or a full OEM tool at 3,000+ units, both on a non-infringing design. Do not accept a supplier's offer to copy a branded enclosure.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What MOQ should I expect for an Anker-style product?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">MOQ starts at 500 units for an existing ODM platform. That tier covers your logo, color and stock packaging. Platform design changes start at 1,000–2,000 units, and a fully custom product with a new mold starts at 3,000 units. The tier depends on who owns the design and whether a new tool is required, not on the product category alone. ODM stock is the lowest-risk way to test a market.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">What lead time should I plan for an Anker-style product?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Plan 25–35 days for ODM stock and 2–4 months for full OEM. ODM stock samples ship in 3–7 days. ODM design development adds 25–45 days for design, then 25–50 days for production depending on packaging and structural changes. Full OEM to your drawings runs 30–60 days for mass production, and a new mold adds 2–3 months, for 2–4 months end to end. Add shipping time to your market.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">How do I verify a supplier matches Anker-level specs?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Ask for five documents before you compare price. Request the cell datasheet, a UN38.3 report naming that cell model, a per-port load test, the factory's own AQL plan and 100% aging logs. Then compare a golden sample against the mass-production batch. Certificates that cover only a "similar model" are not enough. A supplier that cannot name its cell supplier is a trading company or a reseller, not a factory.</p>
</div>
<div class="bg-white rounded-xl p-6 faq-answer">
<h3 class="font-black text-brandBlue mb-2">Is private label cheaper than authorized Anker distribution?</h3>
<p class="text-slate-600 text-sm">Private label usually earns a higher gross margin per unit. At 500 units, certification alone adds $5.00–9.00 per unit, before packaging, inventory and marketing. Authorized distribution has a lower margin and less control, yet it carries less certification and inventory risk. The right choice depends on whether you are building a brand or filling a shelf. Send your target retail price and volume to compare both routes.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</div>
</section>
